import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Kits Are Curated Plugin Lists, Not Implementations","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Kits Are Curated Plugin Lists, Not Implementations","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","status_note":"Proposed. Implementation deferred until this ADR is accepted.","supersedes":["ADR-0034"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0039-kits-are-curated-plugin-lists.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0039-kits-are-curated-plugin-lists.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0039-kits-are-curated-plugin-lists.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0039-kits-are-curated-plugin-lists-not-implementations" tabindex="-1">ADR-0039: Kits Are Curated Plugin Lists, Not Implementations <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0039-kits-are-curated-plugin-lists-not-implementations" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0039: Kits Are Curated Plugin Lists, Not Implementations”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The handl platform has a host-plugin system. The language, runtimes, stdlib, targets, and extensions are all composed of plugins. Kits are curated sets of plugins and other kits. Hosts are agnostic binaries that load a kit and run it.</p><p>That is the intended architecture. It is not what the seven <code>kit-*</code> crates are today.</p><h3 id="what-the-kit-crates-are-today" tabindex="-1">What the <code>kit-*</code> crates are today <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-kit-crates-are-today" aria-label="Permalink to “What the kit-* crates are today”">​</a></h3><p><strong><code>kit-canonical</code></strong> is 2,629 lines holding the entire handl-language implementation: the unified token kind, the unified AST, the lexer, 16 AST converters, a parser behavior, grammar/manifest wiring, and the parse pipeline. The curated plugin list (<code>CanonicalKit::contribute</code>) is ~25 lines at the end. The kit <em>is</em> the implementation, not a curation of it.</p><p><strong><code>kit-hello</code></strong> is the target shape: 66 lines, <code>contribute</code> registers <code>CanonicalKit</code> + <code>HelloLibraryPlugin</code>. Pure curation.</p><p><strong><code>kit-cli</code></strong> is half curation (<code>CliKit</code>), half VM host logic (<code>CliHost</code> — a <code>Host</code> impl wiring ~20 modules, resource limits, <code>new_context</code>).</p><p><strong><code>kit-config</code></strong> and <strong><code>kit-server</code></strong> are pure VM host logic (<code>SandboxHost</code>, <code>ServerHost</code>) with no <code>Plugin</code>, no manifest, no curation. They should be kits curating the sandbox and server plugin sets; instead they are <code>Host</code> structs.</p><p><strong><code>kit-type</code></strong> and <strong><code>kit-type-schema-json</code></strong> are libraries (a type-level VM and a JSON-Schema bridge), not kits. They have no <code>Plugin</code>, no manifest, no curation. <code>kit-type</code> was expected to be a kit that curates type plugins; it is an algorithm.</p><h3 id="why-this-happened" tabindex="-1">Why this happened <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-happened" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this happened”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0034 chose static composition (&quot;zero-cost static dispatch, compile-time trait safety&quot;). That decision <strong>made curation impossible</strong>. A curated list of plugins is meaningless when the composition result is a compile-time enum — you cannot curate a different plugin set and get a different unified kind without recompiling. The static <code>CanonicalKind</code> enum forced all token/AST/ grammar logic into one crate. That crate became <code>kit-canonical</code>, and the kit became the monolith.</p><p>The tiny plugins were already designed for composition — <code>plugin-grammar-call</code> is generic over <code>K: CallToken</code>. But static composition required a single concrete type (<code>CanonicalKind</code>) with 19 trait impls bridging it to every capability. That bridge layer is the monolith.</p><h3 id="what-this-means-for-hosts" tabindex="-1">What this means for hosts <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-means-for-hosts" aria-label="Permalink to “What this means for hosts”">​</a></h3><p>Because kits embed implementation, hosts are coupled to specific kits. Adding a new plugin or building a new kit requires touching host code. Hosts are not agnostic. This blocks the core goal: a host loads any kit and runs it, and adding plugins never touches a host.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="architecture" tabindex="-1">Architecture <a class="header-anchor" href="#architecture" aria-label="Permalink to “Architecture”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Plugins</strong> offer language features, extensions, stdlib, runtime, targets — everything. A plugin is the unit of composition.</p><p><strong>Kits</strong> are curated sets of plugins and other kits. A kit defines what runs where: which language features, which stdlib, which runtime, which target. Examples:</p><ul><li><code>kit-canonical</code> — the handl language (lexer, parser, grammar, AST, eval, …).</li><li><code>kit-config</code> — handl as a configuration language (pure stdlib, no OS, no network, resource-limited).</li><li><code>kit-cli</code> — handl for building CLI tools (OS stdlib, utils, collections).</li><li><code>kit-server</code> — handl for long-running servers (extends <code>kit-config</code> with OS, HTTP, rand, test, otlp).</li><li><code>kit-type</code> — handl&#39;s type system (curates typecheck plugins).</li><li><code>kit-hello</code> — demo (extends <code>kit-canonical</code> with the hello library).</li></ul><p>Kits compose: <code>kit-server</code> extends <code>kit-config</code> extends <code>kit-canonical</code>. A kit is a manifest plus thin registration glue — nothing more.</p><p><strong>Hosts</strong> are agnostic binaries. A host loads a kit and runs it. Adding a plugin, building a new kit, or swapping a runtime never touches host code. Hosts are completely decoupled from what the kit curates.</p><h3 id="composition-is-dynamic" tabindex="-1">Composition is dynamic <a class="header-anchor" href="#composition-is-dynamic" aria-label="Permalink to “Composition is dynamic”">​</a></h3><p>Supersedes ADR-0034&#39;s &quot;zero-cost static dispatch&quot; clause. The composition result is built at kit-load time from the curated plugins, not baked into a compile-time enum. This is what makes curation real: a different kit curates a different plugin set and gets a different language, without recompiling a unified type.</p><h3 id="principle-plugins-offer-capabilities-kits-only-curate" tabindex="-1">Principle: plugins offer capabilities, kits only curate <a class="header-anchor" href="#principle-plugins-offer-capabilities-kits-only-curate" aria-label="Permalink to “Principle: plugins offer capabilities, kits only curate”">​</a></h3><p>Capabilities (lex, parse, eval, fmt, lint, typecheck, build, compile, stdlib modules, …) are offered by plugins. A kit includes whichever plugins it wants; it does not implement them. Kits are pure curation.</p><h3 id="naming-principle" tabindex="-1">Naming principle <a class="header-anchor" href="#naming-principle" aria-label="Permalink to “Naming principle”">​</a></h3><p>Plugin names are kit-agnostic. &quot;canonical&quot; is the name of one kit, not a property of the plugins. A non-handl kit composes its own <code>plugin-token-&lt;lang&gt;</code> / <code>plugin-ast-&lt;lang&gt;</code> instead. No <code>plugin-*-canonical</code> names. Some plugins are language-specific (<code>plugin-ast-handl-value</code>); that is fine — they are reusable across handl-flavored kits, just not across non-handl languages.</p><h3 id="phase-1-—-kit-canonical-becomes-a-thin-kit" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — <code>kit-canonical</code> becomes a thin kit <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-kit-canonical-becomes-a-thin-kit" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — kit-canonical becomes a thin kit”">​</a></h3><p>The 2,629-line <code>lib.rs</code> dissolves. The curated list stays; everything else moves to plugins or the framework:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Logic</th><th>New home</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unified token kind, factory, 19 capability impls</td><td>Per-plugin registry entries in <code>plugin-token-*</code>; the framework composes them dynamically at kit-load time.</td></tr><tr><td>Unified AST node + converters</td><td>Per-shape plugins (<code>plugin-ast-stmt-import</code>, <code>plugin-ast-stmt-export</code>, <code>plugin-ast-stmt-module</code>, <code>plugin-ast-handl-value</code>, existing <code>plugin-ast-*</code>); the framework composes them dynamically.</td></tr><tr><td>Lexer + config</td><td><code>plugin-ext-lexer</code> — a generic lexer that assembles rules from registered <code>plugin-lexer-*</code> plugins.</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypeAnnotRule</code></td><td><code>plugin-grammar-type-annot</code> — one tiny grammar-rule plugin.</td></tr><tr><td>Grammar/manifest wiring, rule registry</td><td>Self-registration in <code>plugin-ext-parser</code> / <code>handl-platform</code>; each <code>plugin-grammar-*</code> registers its rule. No central match statement.</td></tr><tr><td>Parse pipeline (<code>lex</code>/<code>parse</code>/<code>parse_module</code>/<code>grammar_info</code>)</td><td><code>plugin-ext-parser</code> — offers the parse capability; a kit includes it if it wants parsing.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Diagnostic</code> / <code>Diagnostics</code></td><td><code>plugin-diag-core</code> — generic.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Stmt</code> / <code>StmtKind</code> / <code>ModuleOutput</code></td><td>Dissolved into per-statement <code>plugin-ast-stmt-*</code> crates.</td></tr><tr><td><code>CanonicalKit::contribute</code> (the curated list)</td><td><strong>Stays in <code>kit-canonical</code>.</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>kit-canonical</code> shrinks to ~the size of <code>kit-hello</code>.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-kits-and-hosts-are-separated" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — Kits and hosts are separated <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-kits-and-hosts-are-separated" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — Kits and hosts are separated”">​</a></h3><p><strong>Kits become thin curated lists:</strong></p><ul><li><code>kit-config</code> — curates the pure/sandbox plugin set (no OS, no HTTP, no rand).</li><li><code>kit-server</code> — curates the server plugin set (OS + HTTP + rand + test + otlp); extends <code>kit-config</code>.</li><li><code>kit-cli</code> — curates the CLI plugin set; <code>CliKit</code> stays.</li></ul><p><strong><code>Host</code> trait impls are deleted — not moved to new binaries.</strong></p><p><code>SandboxHost</code>, <code>ServerHost</code>, and <code>CliHost</code> are not binaries — they are <code>Host</code> trait impls that wire modules into a <code>ModuleHost</code>. That wiring is exactly what the kit curates. With dynamic composition, a generic host builder reads the kit&#39;s curated <code>plugin-lib-*</code> plugins, registers them into a <code>ModuleHost</code>, applies resource-limit policy, and runs. No per-use-case <code>Host</code> struct needed.</p><ul><li><code>SandboxHost</code> — <strong>deleted.</strong> Its module list becomes <code>kit-config</code>&#39;s curated plugins. Resource limits become host configuration or a policy plugin.</li><li><code>ServerHost</code> — <strong>deleted.</strong> Its module list becomes <code>kit-server</code>&#39;s curated plugins.</li><li><code>CliHost</code> — <strong>deleted.</strong> Its module list becomes <code>kit-cli</code>&#39;s curated plugins.</li></ul><p><strong>Hosts remain generic binaries:</strong></p><ul><li><code>host-cli</code> (exists) — loads any kit, runs it.</li><li><code>host-lsp</code> (exists) — loads any kit, serves LSP.</li><li>No <code>host-sandbox</code>, no <code>host-server</code> — those use cases are <code>kit-config</code> and <code>kit-server</code> loaded by an existing generic host.</li></ul><p>A host loads a kit, dynamically assembles the runtime from the kit&#39;s curated plugins, and runs. The kit is the source of truth for what&#39;s included. Adding a plugin or building a new kit never touches a host.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-kit-type-becomes-a-real-kit" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — <code>kit-type</code> becomes a real kit <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-kit-type-becomes-a-real-kit" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — kit-type becomes a real kit”">​</a></h3><ul><li>NBE type-VM algorithm → <code>plugin-typecheck-nbe</code>.</li><li>JSON-Schema bridge → <code>plugin-typecheck-schema-json</code> (or fold into <code>plugin-lib-type</code>).</li><li><code>kit-type</code> becomes a thin kit curating <code>plugin-typecheck-nbe</code> + <code>plugin-typecheck-core</code> + <code>plugin-lib-type</code> + the schema bridge.</li><li><code>handl-platform</code> and <code>plugin-lib-type</code> depend on the new plugin locations.</li></ul><h3 id="phase-4-—-update-consumers-and-workspace" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — Update consumers and workspace <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-update-consumers-and-workspace" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — Update consumers and workspace”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Update imports in <code>host-cli</code>, <code>host-tooling</code>, <code>host-lsp</code>, <code>plugin-ext-kit</code>, <code>plugin-lsp-*</code>, <code>plugin-typecheck-core</code>, <code>plugin-lib-type</code>, <code>platform</code>.</li><li>Update workspace <code>Cargo.toml</code>.</li><li>Move benches and tests.</li><li>Verify with <code>cargo test --workspace</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Every <code>kit-*</code> crate is a thin, auditable curated list — like <code>kit-hello</code>.</li><li>All logic lives in reusable, composable, replaceable plugins. A new kit composes a different plugin set and gets a different language, without forking or recompiling.</li><li>Hosts are agnostic. Adding a plugin or building a new kit never touches a host. A host loads any kit and runs it.</li><li><code>kit-type</code> becomes what its name promises: a kit curating type plugins.</li><li><code>kit-config</code> and <code>kit-server</code> become kits curating the sandbox and server plugin sets; <code>kit-server</code> extends <code>kit-config</code>. No <code>host-sandbox</code> or <code>host-server</code> binaries — those use cases are kits loaded by a generic host.</li><li>The monolith that grew back into <code>kit-canonical</code> after ADR-0034 is dissolved. ADR-0001&#39;s vision (&quot;a language only includes the node shapes it loads&quot;) is achieved through dynamic composition.</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Crate count increases.</li><li>Wide blast radius: many consumers need import updates and migration from static unified types to dynamic composition.</li><li>Going dynamic supersedes ADR-0034&#39;s static-dispatch clause. The cost is indirect dispatch on token/AST inspection, which is not the hot path (parse/eval are). The framework must be redesigned for object safety (see Implementation notes).</li><li>The framework must gain runtime self-registration registries. This is real framework work, not just code movement.</li><li>Benches and tests need to move and may need adjustment.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-notes" tabindex="-1">Implementation notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-notes" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation notes”">​</a></h2><p>These are engineering problems to solve in service of the architecture, not reasons to question the decision. They are called out here for transparency; detailed design is deferred to implementation.</p><h3 id="object-safety" tabindex="-1">Object safety <a class="header-anchor" href="#object-safety" aria-label="Permalink to “Object safety”">​</a></h3><p><code>TokenKind</code> and <code>Node</code> currently require <code>Clone + PartialEq</code>, which are not object-safe. To support dynamic composition, these traits must be redesigned: remove the <code>Clone</code>/<code>PartialEq</code> supertraits, use <code>Arc&lt;dyn TokenKind&gt;</code> for shared ownership, and provide equality via a trait method (<code>fn eq(&amp;self, other: &amp;dyn TokenKind&gt;) -&gt; bool</code>) rather than <code>PartialEq</code>. The same applies to <code>Node</code>.</p><h3 id="multi-trait-representation" tabindex="-1">Multi-trait representation <a class="header-anchor" href="#multi-trait-representation" aria-label="Permalink to “Multi-trait representation”">​</a></h3><p><code>CanonicalKind</code> currently implements 19 capability traits (<code>CallToken</code>, <code>IndexToken</code>, <code>PipeToken</code>, …). A single <code>Box&lt;dyn TokenKind&gt;</code> cannot represent all 19 simultaneously. Options: a mega-trait combining all capabilities (every plugin depends on all — rejected, not composable), separate boxed trait objects per capability (token duplicated — wasteful), or a trait-object with downcast for capability inspection. The right approach is an open design question for implementation; the architecture requires that it be solved without a static unified enum.</p><h3 id="parser-framework" tabindex="-1">Parser framework <a class="header-anchor" href="#parser-framework" aria-label="Permalink to “Parser framework”">​</a></h3><p><code>plugin-ext-parser</code>&#39;s <code>BasicParser&lt;K: TokenKind&gt;</code>, <code>ParserContext&lt;K&gt;</code>, and <code>ParserBehavior&lt;K&gt;</code> are statically generic. Moving to dynamic composition means rewriting these from <code>K: TokenKind</code> generics to trait-object-based dispatch. This is a framework rewrite, acknowledged as part of the cost.</p><h3 id="self-registration" tabindex="-1">Self-registration <a class="header-anchor" href="#self-registration" aria-label="Permalink to “Self-registration”">​</a></h3><p>Rust has no built-in runtime self-registration. Options: the <code>inventory</code> crate (linker-based, not portable to WASM), explicit registration in <code>contribute</code> (the kit calls <code>ctx.register_plugin(...)</code> for each plugin — same as today, just moved), or build-script codegen. The <code>contribute</code>-based approach is the simplest and most portable; it keeps the kit as the source of truth for curation while the framework provides the dynamic composition machinery.</p><h3 id="ast-inspection-without-pattern-matching" tabindex="-1">AST inspection without pattern matching <a class="header-anchor" href="#ast-inspection-without-pattern-matching" aria-label="Permalink to “AST inspection without pattern matching”">​</a></h3><p>Consumers like <code>host-cli/main.rs</code> currently pattern-match on <code>KitNode::Literal</code> / <code>::Call</code> / <code>::Array</code> / …. With dynamic composition, pattern matching on a unified enum is impossible. Consumers must use a visitor pattern, downcast, or <code>dyn Any</code> inspection. The framework must provide an ergonomic inspection API; the design is deferred to implementation.</p><h3 id="plugin-lib-unification" tabindex="-1"><code>plugin-lib-*</code> unification <a class="header-anchor" href="#plugin-lib-unification" aria-label="Permalink to “plugin-lib-* unification”">​</a></h3><p><code>plugin-lib-*</code> crates currently have stub <code>Plugin</code> impls (<code>// TODO</code>) while real registration is via <code>Module</code> structs + <code>ModuleHost::with()</code>. For kits to curate stdlib plugins, the <code>Plugin</code> impls must be completed so that <code>contribute</code> registers the <code>Module</code> structs into the host. This is a completion task, not a redesign.</p><h3 id="handl-platform-value-model" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-platform</code> value model <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-platform-value-model" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-platform value model”">​</a></h3><p><code>platform/src/value.rs</code> embeds <code>kit-type</code> concrete types (<code>Term</code>, <code>Value</code>, <code>synth</code>, <code>eval</code>). Making the typechecker a replaceable plugin means the platform&#39;s value model must become generic over type representations, not hard-coded to <code>kit-type</code>. This is a deep change scoped as part of Phase 3.</p><h3 id="kit-composition" tabindex="-1">Kit composition <a class="header-anchor" href="#kit-composition" aria-label="Permalink to “Kit composition”">​</a></h3><p><code>KitManifest</code> is currently a flat <code>plugins: Vec&lt;String&gt;</code> with no kit-to-kit composition. The ADR requires that <code>kit-server</code> extends <code>kit-config</code>. This needs either a <code>contribute</code>-chaining mechanism (a kit registers another kit, like <code>kit-hello</code> registers <code>CanonicalKit</code>) or a manifest <code>extends</code> field. The <code>contribute</code>-chaining approach already works today and is the simplest path.</p><h3 id="registration-ordering" tabindex="-1">Registration ordering <a class="header-anchor" href="#registration-ordering" aria-label="Permalink to “Registration ordering”">​</a></h3><p>Lexer rules need ordering (<code>///</code> before <code>//</code>); grammar rules have binding powers. Self-registration must support priority or ordering. The framework registry must accept an ordering hint from each plugin.</p><h3 id="capability-trait-placement" tabindex="-1">Capability trait placement <a class="header-anchor" href="#capability-trait-placement" aria-label="Permalink to “Capability trait placement”">​</a></h3><p><code>PipeToken</code>, <code>HandleToken</code>, <code>MatchToken</code>, etc. are handl-specific capability traits. They live in <code>plugin-grammar-*</code> crates (handl-specific plugins), not in the framework. The framework&#39;s registries must be generic enough to accept plugins with arbitrary capability traits — the design of this genericism is deferred to implementation.</p><h2 id="related-work" tabindex="-1">Related work <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-work" aria-label="Permalink to “Related work”">​</a></h2><ul><li>ADR-0001: Generic Node, Trait-Based Capabilities, and Plugin-Driven Canonical Kit</li><li>ADR-0033: Unified Plugin Crate Naming</li><li>ADR-0034: Zero-Monolith, Plugin-Driven Platform (superseded in part by this ADR&#39;s dynamic-composition decision)</li><li>ADR-0001 (Remaining): Canonical Token/Grammar/Value Plugin Cleanup</li></ul>`,74)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};